<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Flanders</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Flemish</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Flanders is a historic region in the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/low-countries">Low Countries</a>, now largely in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>. A wealthy centre of textile production and trade, it passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> and formed part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire</a>'s possessions in the Low Countries, where it was heavily affected by the wars of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-revolt">Dutch Revolt</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/flanders.jpg" alt="Flanders" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tom Lemmens · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Flanders.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Flanders is mainly flat and incorporates the whole Belgian coast on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a>. The word has had several meanings. It first named the County of Flanders, which existed from the 8th century, in Latin Flandria, until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-first-republic">French First Republic</a> absorbed it, and which until the 1600s also extended over parts of what are now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>. French Flanders, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of Nord, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeelandic-flanders">Zeelandic Flanders</a>, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> province of Zeeland, are historically Flemish regions. In the 19th and 20th centuries the term Flemish, which also came to mean the Dutch language, led people to call the Dutch-speaking part of Belgium Flanders.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a> described the area as the less developed and more warlike part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallia-belgica">Gallia Belgica</a>. The County of Flanders was a feudal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-francia">West Francia</a>. Its first certain count, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baldwin-i-of-flanders">Baldwin I of Flanders</a>, is reported in a document of 862, when he eloped with a daughter of his king, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-bald">Charles the Bald</a>. Cities such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghent">Ghent</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bruges">Bruges</a>, and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antwerp">Antwerp</a> in the Duchy of Brabant, made the region one of the richest and most urbanised parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, weaving the wool of neighbouring lands into cloth, and a sophisticated culture developed there that rivalled that of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>.</p><p>The county was weakened when districts fell under direct French royal rule in the late 12th century, and the remaining parts came under Baldwin V of Hainaut in 1191. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghent">Ghent</a> in 1500 and inherited the Seventeen Provinces in 1506. His Pragmatic Sanction of 1549 made the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/low-countries">Low Countries</a> an entity separate from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> and from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, and on his abdication in 1556 they passed to his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-spain">Philip II of Spain</a>. Antwerp became the second-largest city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a> by 1560.</p><p>In 1830 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgian-revolution">Belgian Revolution</a> split <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-london-1839">Treaty of London</a> of 1839 confirmed Belgian independence. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> became an official language in public secondary schools in 1873, and in 1898 Dutch and French were declared equal in laws and royal orders. The first Flemish university opened in 1930.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Flanders is the Dutch-speaking northern part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>. The Flemish Region and the Flemish Community form one body with a parliament and government, as constitutional institutions of the Kingdom of Belgium. The official capital is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a>, which lies in its own region. Flanders covers 45 per cent of the territory of Belgium but holds more than half its population.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Flanders” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Flanders</b>’ founder, Baldwin Iron Arm, won his county by eloping with Judith, a Carolingian princess who had already been married to two kings of Wessex, father and son.</blockquote><a href="/empire/county-of-flanders">County of Flanders · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>When Richilde and her son lost <b>Flanders</b> after the Battle of Cassel in 1071, Hainaut survived as a separate county under her descendants, centred on Mons and Valenciennes.</blockquote><a href="/empire/county-of-hainaut">County of Hainaut · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Fine wool, shipped from the Cantabrian ports by the merchants of Burgos to <b>Flanders</b>, became Castile's leading export, and the fairs of Medina del Campo made it a centre of European finance.</blockquote><a href="/empire/crown-of-castile">Crown of Castile · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Henry I (d. 1235) spent the next half century enlarging it, founding towns such as 's-Hertogenbosch around 1185, competing with <b>Flanders</b>, Holland and the prince-bishops of Liège, and building a network of castles and chartered towns that gave the duchy its distinctively urban character.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-brabant">Duchy of Brabant · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Military power grew dramatically under the Valois dukes, who could draw on the wealth of <b>Flanders</b> and the Low Countries as well as the duchy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-burgundy">Duchy of Burgundy · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Merchants and bankers traded through Genoa and across the Alpine passes to Switzerland, Germany and <b>Flanders</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-milan">Duchy of Milan · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
